+This experimental bridge executes ordinary ARM libc memory-library calls with
+the host's native libc. It remains off unless `MACLATOR_NATIVE_PLATFORM=1` or
+`--native-platform` is supplied. It does not modify applications, system
+libraries, the benchmark, or the shared Maclator candidate.
+
+Build this independent runtime with Cargo, then run `bash platform/build.sh`.
+Set `MACLATOR_PLATFORM_SHIM` to the resulting absolute `libmclplatform.dylib`
+path, or place the dylib next to the experimental runtime. The ARM dylib has
+four `DYLD_INTERPOSE` entries for memcpy, memmove, memset and bzero. It has no
+constructors, Foundation/ObjC dependencies, allocation or TLS initialization.
+
+Calls shorter than 4096 bytes tail-call the original ARM library, except copy
+calls whose destination and source have different top-byte tags. Those small
+copies also use the host bridge so overlap direction uses normalized pointers.
+Larger calls use `svc #0x80`, x16=0x4d4d and x17=1/2/3 for copy/fill/zero. Arguments retain
+the original C ABI: copy/fill take x0/x1/x2; bzero takes x0/x1. Success sets
+x17=0 and leaves x0 as the original destination, including its tag. Declined
+calls set x17=1 and tail-call the original guest implementation. x30 and SP are
+never changed by the leaf shim. The host handler preserves NZCV, all remaining
+guest registers, FP state and TLS state; it saves/restores host errno. Guest
+errno is not translated or set. Actual writes to a buffer containing errno are
+ordinary requested memory writes, rather than an errno side effect.
+
+Both copy symbols use one shared replacement and native memmove. Dyld may
+resolve memcpy and memmove to the same implementation address, so separate
+interpose targets would be ambiguous. Memmove implements every valid memcpy
+call and also handles normalized tagged pointers that refer to overlapping
+memory. Memset truncates its integer argument to the low eight bits. Zero-size
+calls do not inspect either pointer. Calls exceeding the host object-size bound,
+arithmetic overflow, null nonempty ranges and noncanonical host addresses are
+declined before native access.
+
+The handler checks each entire normalized interval. Commpage sources can be
+translated only when fully contained in its linear span. Crossing either span
+boundary is declined. All destinations intersecting the commpage are declined,
+including in-place mappings, so the original guest access retains its existing
+protection/fault behavior. ARM child launches forward the explicit opt-in and
+shim path; native children have the ARM shim removed from their environment.
+Stats under `MACLATOR_STATS=1` distinguish completed copy/fill calls, bytes and
+declines. Interposing may be denied by dyld/AMFI and cannot cover compiler
+inlining or every private/directly-bound libc call; counters establish actual
+coverage instead of assuming it.
+
+Checked address arithmetic does **not** establish VM permissions. The prototype
+requires valid live C objects for the duration of native access, as the original
+library function does. It does not yet provide recoverable guest synchronous
+faults for bad pointers in a host libc call, and must not be advertised as
+synchronously fault-safe. It preserves existing host fault diagnostics. No
+allocation, Mach RPC or host framework call is used in the hot copy/fill path.
+
+The original guest libc path failed a positive overlapping copy with different
+top-byte tags in both disabled and initial enabled modes (length 15, offset +1).
+Its pointer-ordering behavior is an observed limitation of the existing guest
+path; without an ARM-hardware comparison this is not attributed to a translator
+bug. Keep the initial failure logs. The enabled prototype now routes all such
+nonzero differing-tag copies through checked normalized native memmove. The
+common native/off/on comparison uses matching tags for overlap, while an
+enabled-only real-symbol probe covers differing tags at 1..4095 boundary sizes
+and 4096, positive/negative overlaps and differently tagged identical addresses.
+Other small memory calls still use the original guest library.
+
+Run the Rust contract tests with `cargo test -p maclator platform::tests -j 1`
+using an independent target directory. Run the ARM/native end-to-end probe:
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/check_native_platform.py \
+  --runtime /absolute/path/to/experimental/maclator \
+  --sysroot /absolute/path/to/arm64/sysroot \
+  --output /absolute/path/to/probe-results
+```
+
+The same C source and real library calls run on native x86, translated ARM with
+the bridge disabled, and translated ARM with it enabled, including redirected
+commpage mode. Tests cover zero/boundary sizes, alignments, tagged return values,
+overlap in both directions, canaries, read-only sources, guard pages, guest errno,
+raw private-call ABI/flags/decline behavior, ARM child option forwarding and
+native child shim scrubbing. The runner verifies native byte checksums and
+requires enough completed library calls to distinguish real symbol interception
+from its few explicit ABI probes. Each process group has a bounded deadline.
+These are correctness/compatibility tests; report a separate alternating A/B of
+the unchanged workload before claiming any performance gain or 95% target.
+
+Primary dyld source for the interpose section and self-call exclusions:
+[Apple dyld-interposing.h](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/dyld/blob/main/include/mach-o/dyld-interposing.h)
+and [Apple DyldRuntimeState.cpp](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/dyld/blob/main/dyld/DyldRuntimeState.cpp).
